Lab 15: Using Ansible Filters and Plugins
Objectives

By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

    Understand the purpose and functionality of Ansible filters and plugins
    Apply built-in filters to manipulate and transform data in Ansible playbooks
    Create and use custom filters for specific data transformation needs
    Implement lookup plugins to interact with external systems and data sources
    Use callback plugins to customize Ansible output and logging
    Troubleshoot common issues with filters and plugins

Prerequisites

Before starting this lab, you should have:

    Basic understanding of Ansible playbooks, tasks, and variables
    Familiarity with YAML syntax and Jinja2 templating
    Knowledge of Linux command line operations
    Understanding of Python basics (helpful but not required)
    Experience with Ansible inventory and configuration files

Install the required software on your lab machine (CentOS/RHEL 8 or Ubuntu 20.04 LTS with Python 3.8+):

python3 -m pip install --user --upgrade pip
python3 -m pip install --user ansible requests
ansible --version

You should see ansible [core 2.11+] and a Python 3.8+ interpreter reported in the version output.
