Expected Outcomes

    Working playbooks that demonstrate string, list, dictionary, date, and math filters, plus a custom filter plugin performing string reversal, number extraction, percentage calculation, and byte formatting
    A functioning custom lookup plugin that reads structured JSON data and a custom callback plugin that produces timestamped, per-task execution logs
    A combined infrastructure reporting playbook that loads external JSON data, applies filters to compute summaries, and raises threshold-based alerts

Troubleshooting

    If ansible-playbook custom-filters.yml fails with an error like "no filter named 'reverse_string'", investigate: is the command being run from the ~/ansible-filters-lab directory, and does filter_plugins/custom_filters.py pass python3 -m py_compile filter_plugins/custom_filters.py without errors?
    If the callback plugin produces no extra output when running callback-test.yml, check whether ANSIBLE_STDOUT_CALLBACK was actually exported in the same shell session as the ansible-playbook command, and whether callback_plugins/ sits alongside ansible.cfg in the current working directory.

Conclusion

In this lab you built a complete working set of Ansible filters and plugins: string, list, dictionary, date, and math filter demonstrations; a custom filter module providing string reversal, numeric extraction, percentage calculation, and byte formatting; a custom lookup plugin for reading JSON data; and a custom callback plugin for detailed execution logging. You then combined all of these into a single infrastructure reporting playbook that loaded external data, computed summary statistics, and raised threshold-based alerts, and you finished by writing a diagnostic playbook to verify filter behavior directly. Through this process you practiced extending Ansible's Jinja2-based templating with custom Python logic, a core skill for building maintainable, data-driven automation. From here, consider exploring the official Ansible documentation on writing plugins and the Jinja2 Template Designer documentation to deepen your understanding of filter chaining and plugin APIs, and try packaging your custom plugins into an Ansible Collection for reuse across projects.
